Digital Internet Tunnel V1.0

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Uses encryption and authentication to make the Internet a secure, private
   network; lower cost than leased lines

o  Eliminates need to modify IP-networked applications for security

o  Group Tunnel V1.0 connects remote LANs

o  Personal Tunnel V1.0 connects a remote PC to a Group Tunnel

o  Supports enVISN architecture

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

The Digital Internet Tunnel turns the Internet, or other TCP/IP network,
into a secure, private network. Tunneling allows information to be passed
between two private networks over a public network as if the two were
connected by a single, physical wire. Authentication controls ensure that
the tunnel is established between authorized systems. Data, encrypted by
an authorized client tunnel system, is encapsulated into unrecognizable
TCP/IP data packets and sent across the network to an authorized tunnel
server. The server receives the packets and decrypts the data. The
transmitted information is then available over the private network, just
like other corporate data. The process also work in reverse when
information is sent from the tunnel server to individual and group
servers outside the corporate network.

Features

o  Multi-level authentication --RSA public key technology authenticates the
   systems and establishes the tunnel. Second, the tunnel switches to
   RC4-based secret key technology for bulk data encryption. During data
   transmission over the Internet, the tunnel client and tunnel endpoint
   server regularly pass new encryption keys back and forth to decode
   transmitted data, making it virtually impossible to break the encryption
   code. The Internet Tunnel in the U.S. uses 128-bit key encryption, while
   the international Internet Tunnel supports 40-bit key. When a
   multi-national tunnel is requested, arbitration reconciles the
   discrepancy transparently and lets both systems operate in a common key
   mode.

o  Tunnel management -- TCP/IP network protocol manages transmission flow
   control and delivery. This process frees the Internet Tunnel software to
   focus on ensuring the safe, secure transmission of data entering and
   leaving the tunnel. The Digital Internet Group Tunnel manages tunnel
   functions, eliminating the need for a dedicated administrator. In group
   tunnel environments, either one of the conversant servers performs the
   required tasks. Internet Tunnel management capabilities include

   - Connections and authorization
   - Public key generation
   - Tunnel parameters
   - DHCP-style address assignment to tunnel clients
   - Tunnel-related routing change monitoring
   - Tunnel deletion

o  Firewall independence and compatibility -- The Internet Tunnel's seamless
   compatibility with most firewalls provides a high level of protection for
   information during its transit over the Internet. To provide additional
   security, the Digital Internet Group Tunnel, with the encryption keys, is
   firewall independent.  That is, although users have the option of
   combining the tunnel and firewall on a single system, they can also
   choose to run the tunnel on a second system behind the firewall,
   protected from the Internet. This additional security is the preferred
   choice and Digital provides it at a cost equal to other vendor's
   single-system solutions.

o  enVISN Architecture -- Digital Internal Tunnel supports enVISN, the
   network architecture and product strategy that combines virtual LAN
   technology, distributed routing, and high-speed switching with
   centralized, policy-based administration. enVISN's flexible networks can
   be customized.

The Digital Internet Tunnel comes in two versions; Digital Internet
Personal Tunnel V1.0 and Digital Internet Group Tunnel V1.0.

Digital Internet Personal Tunnel V1.0

The Digital Internet Personal Tunnel is designed to be used by a
telecommuter or frequent traveler to establish a tunnel over the Internet
between a PC and the corporate private network. The Personal Tunnel must
connect with a Group Tunnel server which performs tunnel authorization,
authentication, and management, and allows the user's data to pass
through the private network's firewall.

The Digital Internet Personal Tunnel, based on Microsoft's TCP/IP stack,
(an integral part of the Microsoft Windows 95 and Windows NT operating
systems), allows users equipped with Microsoft TCP/IP-compliant Intel PCs
to connect to a Group Tunnel server. The Internet Personal Tunnel
includes

o  Windows 95 PC tunnel client software with installation and tunnel
   management capabilities

o  Windows 95 GUI-based tunnel setup and administration

o  Windows 95 pseudo-adapter device driver

Digital Internet Group Tunnel V1.0

The Group Tunnel software runs on Alpha systems with the Digital UNIX
operating system and supports multiple concurrent tunnels and
organization-to-organization communications such as manufacturer/supplier
EDI. It works the same way as the Personal Tunnel, except that a remote
group tunnel server running the Digital Internet Group Tunnel software
connects with the recipient corporate network server running the same
software. The components are

o  Digital UNIX group tunnel server and management software

o  Digital UNIX tunnel pseudo device driver

o  Digital UNIX installation and configuration support

BENEFITS

o  Secure connections -- Gives individuals users and groups an
   authenticated, secure electronic connection to their private network over
   the Internet.

o  Cost savings -- Frees IS managers from the burden of building and
   managing private networks, saves network management, operations, and
   long-distance, leased-line costs

o  Firewall compatibility -- Enhances the connectivity security, and
   protects assets on a private network and sensitive data on the public
   network.

o  Firewall independence -- Encryption keys are not stored on the firewall;
   a system directly connected to the public Internet

o  Internationally approved and supported

AVAILABILITY AND ORDERING

The Digital Internet Group Tunnel V1.0 and the Digital Internet Personal
Tunnel V1.0 will be available in the U.S. and Canada on November 15,
1995. The international versions will be available on December 31, 1995.

Evaluation kits will be available at reduced cost for testing evaluation
purposes only. This software can be used for a limited time only.

